                                    Rules for identifying java identifiers
1) The only allowed characters in java identifiers are
        a to z
        A to Z
        0 to 9
        $
        _
If we are using any other characters we will get compile time error

2) Identifiers cannot start from digits
        total123 ---> Valid
        123total ---> Invalid

3) Java identifiers are case sensitive. Ofcourse java language itself is treated as case sensitive language.
class test1{
    int number = 10;
    int Number = 20;
    int NUMBER = 30;
}

4) There is no length limit for java identifiers but it is not recommended to take too lengthy identifiers.

5) We cant use reserved words as identifiers
    int x = 10;
    int if = 20; Here if is a reserved word so we cannot use this. So it is invalid.

6) All predefined java class names and interface names we can use as identifiers
class test2{
    public static void main(String[] args){
        int String = 10;                |   int Runnable = 999;
        System.out.println(String);     |   System.out.println(Runnable);
    }
}
Even though both of them are valid it is not good practice because it reduces readability and creates confusion.

